        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 1473,
            "sid": 3,
            "position_x": 729,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 686,
            "height": 631,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "log10 asks: 10 to the power of what gives this value? It is the logarithm base 10.\n\n1000 comes out as 3, 100 as 2, 1 as 0, 0.1 as -1. It counts the digits, roughly:\neach factor of TEN in the input adds 1 to the output. In the demo the sine wave runs from 0.1 to 10.1; log10 spreads the stretch from 0.1 to 1 over as much of the plot as the stretch from 1 to 10.\n\nUse it to tame a value that spans many sizes - a sound level, a distance, a count - into an even scale. Decibels are 20 times the log10 of an amplitude ratio.\n\nLists, NumPy arrays and PyTorch tensors go through element by element, keeping their shape. In the NumPy example at the bottom left, a random array (left) goes through log10 cell by cell (right).\n\nSYNTAX:\nlog10\n\nEXAMPLES:\nlog10 then * 20     decibels from an amplitude ratio\nlog10      how many digits a count has\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe data to be operated on. Receiving data here triggers the calculation.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresult:\nThe base 10 logarithm of the input.\n\nZERO AND NEGATIVE NUMBERS:\nThese have no logarithm. A single number of zero or less gives -inf (negative infinity) rather than stopping the patch. Lists, arrays and tensors take the logarithm of the absolute value instead, so -100 gives 2 and only 0 gives -inf. The demo adds 0.1 to the wave so it never reaches zero.\n\nSEE ALSO:\nlog2    logarithm base 2\nexp     e to the power of the input\n^       raise to a power",
                    "value_type": "string"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 678,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 615,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "string"
                }
            },
            "text": "log10 asks: 10 to the power of what gives this value? It is the logarithm base 10.\n\n1000 comes out as 3, 100 as 2, 1 as 0, 0.1 as -1. It counts the digits, roughly:\neach factor of TEN in the input adds 1 to the output. In the demo the sine wave runs from 0.1 to 10.1; log10 spreads the stretch from 0.1 to 1 over as much of the plot as the stretch from 1 to 10.\n\nUse it to tame a value that spans many sizes - a sound level, a distance, a count - into an even scale. Decibels are 20 times the log10 of an amplitude ratio.\n\nLists, NumPy arrays and PyTorch tensors go through element by element, keeping their shape. In the NumPy example at the bottom left, a random array (left) goes through log10 cell by cell (right).\n\nSYNTAX:\nlog10\n\nEXAMPLES:\nlog10 then * 20     decibels from an amplitude ratio\nlog10      how many digits a count has\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe data to be operated on. Receiving data here triggers the calculation.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresult:\nThe base 10 logarithm of the input.\n\nZERO AND NEGATIVE NUMBERS:\nThese have no logarithm. A single number of zero or less gives -inf (negative infinity) rather than stopping the patch. Lists, arrays and tensors take the logarithm of the absolute value instead, so -100 gives 2 and only 0 gives -inf. The demo adds 0.1 to the wave so it never reaches zero.\n\nSEE ALSO:\nlog2    logarithm base 2\nexp     e to the power of the input\n^       raise to a power"
        },
